“I’ve never spoken on this with anybody because I always felt like it’s similar to a magician explaining how he does his tricks. Like why are people over-explaining how they make sh&#8211; that’s good?” he said when asked directly if he used AI music programs like Suno and compared its current controversial usage to that of the use of Auto-Tune during the mid-2000s. &#8220;It’s supposed to give you an experience and a feeling. But we definitely used AI as a [tool],&#8221; he continued. &#8220;And I know people are going to be mad about me saying this, but it’s where technology is going. It’s no different than when Auto-Tune came out. Some people were opposed to it, but real artists took it and used it.&#8221;

He added: “But as far as the writing, all the vocals are all me. There’s nothing wrong with using technology as a tool.&#8221;

The AI allegations really started earlier this summer after Shoreline Mafia&#8217;s Fenix Flexin had everyone talking with his own &#8217;80s-inspired single &#8220; RUBBERZ ,&#8221; and then footage popped up on social media of he and Tyga in the studio recording a song titled &#8220; Lavish &#8221; that had those very same &#8217;80s-pop-leaning synth elements.

However, according to Tyga, the two just so happened to be &#8220;experimenting&#8221; with this new sound at the same time. &#8220;Nah, we just did the &#8216;Lavish&#8217; record. But when he came to the studio, I was already deep into working on my ‘80s-sounding music. I had already played him some stuff,&#8221; he replied after being asked if he and Fenix planned any of this. &#8220;So, when I saw what he was doing, I was like, &#8216;Oh, this is fire! We should do this record together because I see you on a similar wave. I think it was just cool to do, you know? We’re going to put the video out in a few weeks. The song just didn’t fit the aesthetic of the  $tarface project. &#8220;
